# 🐧 Linux & Parrot Security OS

## Overview

Linux is an important part of my cybersecurity learning environment. I use command-line utilities to navigate the filesystem, inspect files, investigate network configuration, and troubleshoot system issues.

## Commands and What They Do

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `pwd` | Display the current directory |
| `ls -la` | List files, including hidden files |
| `cd` | Navigate between directories |
| `cat file.txt` | Display a text file |
| `grep` | Search text for matching patterns |
| `find` | Search for files and directories |
| `chmod` | Change file permissions |
| `tar` | Create or extract archives |
| `ip addr` | Inspect interfaces and IP addresses |
| `ip route` | Display routing information |
| `ip neigh` | View the local network neighbor table |
| `wget` | Download files from URLs |

## Example Workflow

```bash
pwd
ls -la
ip addr
ip route
```

This sequence helps me inspect my working directory, review files, identify network interfaces, and understand routing configuration.

## What I Learned

- Linux uses permissions to control access to files and directories.
- Hidden files may contain important configuration information.
- The terminal is useful for system administration and troubleshooting.
- Network commands help identify addressing and routing issues.
- Administrative privileges should be used carefully.

